[
  {
    "state": "We are most grateful for the privilege of setting before you the Kestrel Analytics Cardmember Courtesy Terms, kindly revised on 12 January 2024, and we hope the following excerpt will be of gentle assistance to every reader who has honoured us with a question.\n\nSection 1. A billing question is always welcome. A formal dispute, however, must reach our Courtesy Desk within sixty calendar days after the statement date printed on the monthly summary. Correspondence arriving on the sixtieth day is still treated as timely. Correspondence arriving on the sixty-first day, much as we regret it, cannot be accepted under this section.\n\nSection 2. Once a dispute is accepted as timely, we are pleased to place a provisional credit equal to the disputed amount minus a four-dollar courtesy fee. If the disputed amount is twenty-five dollars or less, that fee is waived and the provisional credit equals the disputed amount in full. The fee is never waived merely because one line among several is twenty-five dollars or less.\n\nSection 3. Please disregard, with our apologies for any confusion, the welcome booklet illustration of a ninety-day window. That illustration governs prepaid travel cards only. A footnote on the booklet second page repeats ninety days. It does not amend this credit-card excerpt, and we kindly ask that it not be applied here.\n\nSection 4. Rosa Okafor, whom we thank most sincerely, wrote on 28 April 2024 about the statement dated 1 March 2024. She asked, with notable politeness, that two March postings be reviewed: an 18.00 dollar Reed and Willow charge posted 4 March, which she considers unauthorized, and an 86.40 dollar Mossgray Goods charge posted 9 March, which she considers a duplicate of a settled 86.40 dollar purchase dated 2 February. She expressly did not seek reversal of the February purchase. She asked only that the March duplicate and the unauthorized March item be reversed.\n\nSection 5. Her March summary also lists a foreign-transaction assessment of 0.43 dollars on an unrelated cafe purchase of 86.00 dollars, and interest of 3.12 dollars on an undisputed prior balance. She has not questioned either figure. Foreign-transaction assessments are never disputable. Interest on an undisputed prior balance remains even if a dispute is later upheld. Interest that accrued solely because of an upheld charge would, in that event, be removed. No such interest figure is stated for these two March items.\n\nSection 6. A sample letter printed at the front of this packet, dated 15 June 2023, concerns a different cardholder and a statement dated 1 January 2023, and is offered only as a model of courteous wording. It must not be read as describing Ms Okafor dates or amounts. We remain, as always, most obliged for your kind attention and we thank you for reading with such patience.",
    "questions": [
      {
        "type": "binary",
        "instruction": "Dispute received in time?",
        "target": true,
        "hard_cases": ["long_state", "temporal_reasoning", "distractor", "evidence_middle"],
        "notes": "1 Mar 2024 plus 60 days is 30 Apr; letter 28 Apr; inside. Ninety-day booklet does not apply."
      },
      {
        "type": "multiclass",
        "instruction": "Provisional credit figure?",
        "candidates": [
          {"id": "credit_18", "description": "Eighteen dollars"},
          {"id": "credit_100_40", "description": "One hundred dollars and forty cents"},
          {"id": "credit_104_40", "description": "One hundred four dollars and forty cents"},
          {"id": "credit_none", "description": "No provisional credit"}
        ],
        "target": "credit_100_40",
        "hard_cases": ["long_state", "numeric_reasoning", "exception", "evidence_middle"],
        "notes": "18.00 plus 86.40 is 104.40; total over 25 so fee 4 applies; 104.40 minus 4 is 100.40."
      },
      {
        "type": "multilabel",
        "instruction": "Charges she asked reversed?",
        "candidates": [
          {"id": "unauthorized_march", "description": "March unauthorized charge"},
          {"id": "march_duplicate", "description": "March duplicate charge"},
          {"id": "february_purchase", "description": "February settled purchase"},
          {"id": "foreign_assessment", "description": "Foreign transaction assessment"},
          {"id": "prior_interest", "description": "Prior-balance interest"}
        ],
        "target": ["unauthorized_march", "march_duplicate"],
        "hard_cases": ["long_state", "negation", "distractor", "evidence_middle"],
        "notes": "She asked only the March unauthorized item and March duplicate reversed, not February or other figures."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "state": "We wish, most respectfully and with our warmest apologies, to share this postmortem of the Courtesy Desk interruption that touched a handful of card disputes in early May 2024. We are truly sorry for any unease, and we thank every partner who waited so patiently.\n\nOpening note. A bulletin pinned above this memorandum, timed 08:00 on 4 May, states that every dispute received in May remains closed. We kindly ask readers to treat that bulletin as superseded. It was prepared before the restoration steps below and does not describe the final position. A later sticky note repeating the word closed is likewise withdrawn.\n\nBackground. Larkspur Supply, whose patience we value deeply, was debited a chargeback of 410.00 dollars on 12 April 2024 in connection with invoice LS-19 and cardholder Tomasz Moreau. On 18 April Mr Moreau wrote, with conspicuous courtesy, that invoice LS-19 was entirely correct and that he did not wish to dispute it. On 29 April Larkspur Supply asked us, most politely, to reverse that chargeback. We completed the reversal on 30 April, returning 410.00 dollars to the merchant. No further sum is outstanding on LS-19 in either direction.\n\nSeparate matter. On 1 May 2024 Mr Moreau filed a dispute of 410.00 dollars against Bloom and Twine Florist. The statement date for that charge is 15 March 2024. The filing reached us the same day, 1 May. Our rule, restated here only for clarity, is that a dispute is timely if received within sixty calendar days after the statement date. We mention a different cardholder, whose 410.00 dollar travel dispute was filed ninety days after a January statement, only so that staff will not confuse the two files. That travel file was declined and is closed. It is not part of Mr Moreau's florist matter.\n\nInterruption. On Friday 3 May the queue stalled after a courteous but faulty maintenance window. An automated tidy-up on Saturday 4 May marked the florist dispute closed. That mark was an error. On Monday 6 May a specialist reopened the florist dispute, and it remained open at the time of this note on Tuesday 7 May. No provisional credit had yet been posted to it. A hypothetical remark in the engineer chat, which we quote only to be complete, said that if the reopen had failed the cardholder would have had to file again. The reopen did not fail.\n\nWe thank Larkspur Supply and Mr Moreau, most sincerely, for their forbearance, and we remain entirely at your service.",
    "questions": [
      {
        "type": "binary",
        "instruction": "Florist case remains closed?",
        "target": false,
        "hard_cases": ["long_state", "contradiction", "multi_turn", "evidence_end"],
        "notes": "4 May auto-close and opening bulletin were superseded; reopened 6 May and still open 7 May."
      },
      {
        "type": "binary",
